The memory palace asks where it lives before it writes anything
=================================================================

A palace is a folder of markdown — wings, rooms, drawers — with no database and no format of its own.
That is the whole point: readable, greppable, puttable in git, backable-up like any directory. But the
app had never asked where that folder should be. It went to `<data>/palace`, or wherever an environment
variable pointed, and the person whose memory it was had no say.

Now:

  * **`palace_root()` reads the preference first**, then `QUIXI_PALACE_DIR`, then the default. A folder
    somebody picked at setup is a folder they expect to stay picked — an environment variable set by a
    launcher should not silently outrank it.
  * **`Memory.info`** reports the real path, drawer/wing/room counts and byte size. A screen asking
    where to put your memory has to be able to say how much memory that is.
  * **The newcomer path asks.** After the model question and before the first chat: the folder, how it
    is kept (append-only sealed archive, or an ordinary folder you can delete from), and what protects
    it (whole-disk, an age/GPG archive, or nothing — with the warning stated once and not repeated).
  * **The old-hand table gets the same two rows**, because a quick setup is not a lesser answer to the
    same question.

Two things this deliberately does not do, and the screen says so rather than implying otherwise:
sealed mode is recorded, not yet enforced (`chattr +a` needs root at setup time), and encryption is
advice — the app records which of the three you picked and warns about the third. It does not encrypt
anything itself.

